From 87f401be704525d3ffb425c797323c3e6c66a90b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 13:35:46 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 13/27] tests: mdb_env_set_cachedrop on a generated environment
 of large values

A table of 200 values of 10,000 bytes, three 4 KB overflow pages each, as
monerod stores transaction data. The tests read the page cache through
mincore() on the environment's own map.

- Its arguments: EINVAL for an environment not open, during a write
  transaction, and with MDB_WRITEMAP; on and off twice.
- Freed pages: half the values are deleted while a reader on the earlier
  snapshot is open. Their pages stay cached, and the reader reads the
  values intact, for as long as the reader lives. After it ends, the next
  commit drops at least 90% of them, and none of the pages of the values
  that are kept.
- Written overflow pages: with a long delay, the runs a commit writes stay.
  With no delay, the commit after the one that wrote them drops at least
  90% of them. The values then read back intact from the file.
- 300 commits of random puts, overwrites and deletes, with a reader that
  lives across a few commits now and then. Every value reads back intact,
  every 50 commits and after reopening the environment.

The residency tests skip where the cache drop is not available (ENOSYS),
where LMDB's page size is not the OS's, and where the file system does not
let pages go (tmpfs). Checked against two broken versions of the cache
drop: one that drops freed pages a reader can still see fails the freed
pages test; one that is never on fails both residency tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
